The dark of hearing might be the moment the woods go quiet. You could hear the insects a second ago. Now you can hear your own breath, and you’re waiting for the sound that explains why they stopped.

The dark of smell might be coming home and finding that your house smells like nothing. You can see your couch, your shoes, the blanket on the chair. But the little bit of evidence that tells you I’m home is gone.

And each sense has its own version of glare. A waterfall can hide footsteps. Bleach can erase a room’s familiar smell. A blinding white light can hide as much as a blackout. You can make one thing disappear by giving the sense too little to work with, or by giving it too much of something else.

Sense	What could feel like its dark
Sight	The hallway light goes out halfway down.
Hearing	The footsteps behind you stop when you stop.
Smell	A familiar jacket has lost the scent of the person who wore it.
Touch	You reach for the banister where it’s always been and close your hand on air.
Balance	You’re lying still and suddenly feel the bed tilt.

The flute gives you a way to play with this. Hold a note until the listener expects it to continue, then let the pitch disappear while the breath keeps going. The room still has sound in it. What vanished was the thing the listener was following.

That’s a Pantheon experiment you could run across senses. Give somebody enough evidence to feel oriented, then take away one cue. See whether they miss it, whether another sense fills the gap, and what they imagine is there in the meantime. Leave the hallway lit. Let them see all the way down it. Then take away the sound of their own footsteps.